Make every pty descriptor node-pty hands back close-on-exec.

openpty(3), forkpty(3) and posix_openpt(3) open the master without O_CLOEXEC, and node-pty
1.1.0 only ever sets O_NONBLOCK on it. So the master of every terminal stays open across
exec in the embedding process, and every program it starts afterwards inherits all of them:
a child spawned through Node's child_process (libuv closes only what it opened itself,
which it opens close-on-exec), and the shell of the NEXT terminal node-pty forks. A master
is the keyboard of the shell on its far end, so any of those programs could type into any
open terminal -- and bubblewrap passes an inherited descriptor straight into a sandbox.
Measured before this patch, from a plain Node process with one terminal open: both a
child_process child and a second node-pty shell listed `20 -> /dev/ptmx`.

FD_CLOEXEC is set on the master in both of pty.fork's branches and on both ends pty.open
returns. pty.open's far end is handed to a child as its standard streams by whoever opened
it, and a dup2 onto 0-2 makes copies that are inheritable whatever the original was.

Set after the fact rather than at open, because openpty/forkpty take no flags. That leaves
no window a JavaScript spawn can fall into: the flag is set before the call returns, and
forkpty's own child closes its copy before it execs.

1.2.0's betas mark descriptors close-on-exec in the CHILD of their own fork only, which
does nothing for a child_process child; not upstreamed yet.

